.TH LZOP 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
lzop, unlzop, lzopcat \- compress or expand files in lzop format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lzop
.RB [ \-cdfkqtUv123456CF ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B unlzop
.RB [ \-cfkqtUvF ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B lzopcat
.RB [ \-ftF ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lzop
compresses each
.I file
with the LZO1X algorithm, or with
.B \-d
decompresses it.
.B unlzop
is
.BR "lzop \-d" ,
and
.B lzopcat
is
.BR "lzop \-dc" .
All three accept the same options.
.PP
When compressing, the output file is
.I file
with
.B .lzo
appended, even when it already ends in
.BR .lzo .
When decompressing, a final
.B .lzo
is removed, and any other name gets
.B .out
appended.
The input file is kept unless
.B \-U
is given.
An existing output file is an error unless
.B \-f
is given.
The output file gets the permissions of the input file, and the current
time as its modification time.
.PP
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
.B lzop
reads standard input and writes standard output.
It refuses to read from a console, even when compressing, unless
.B \-f
is given.
.PP
Data is compressed in blocks of 256 KiB, and a block that does not
shrink is stored uncompressed.
Each block carries an Adler-32 checksum of its uncompressed data, which
is verified when decompressing.
The header records no file name, permissions, or time, and these fields
are ignored in files from other lzop programs.
Decompression accepts files compressed at any level, and also verifies
CRC-32 checksums.
.PP
Only the first compressed stream in a file is read, and anything after
it is silently ignored.
A concatenation of
.B .lzo
files, such as the output of
.B "lzop \-c"
with several files, expands to the first file only.
With
.BR \-U ,
the input file is still deleted, and the rest of its data is lost.
.SS Diagnostics
Invalid input is reported with one of these messages:
.TP
.B bad magic number
The input is not in lzop format.
.TP
.BI "header_error " n
The header is invalid or unsupported.
2 means a header checksum mismatch;
3, a format older than lzop 0.94, or a bad extra field;
\-1, 14, and 15, an unknown method or level;
16, a file that requires lzop later than 1.01 or uses a filter;
and \-13, reserved flags set.
.TP
.BR "corrupted data" ", " "checksum error"
A block is damaged.
.TP
.B short read
The input ends early, or reading it fails.
.TP
.B this file is a split lzop file
Split files are not supported.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-dc ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options.
Level options have no effect when decompressing.
.TP
.B \-1
Compress with the LZO1X-1(15) method.
.TP
.BR \-2 " to " \-6
Compress with the LZO1X-1 method, the default.
These levels are identical.
.TP
.BR \-7 ", " \-8 ", " \-9
Fail with the message
.BR "high compression not compiled in" .
The output file, if any, has already been created, and is left empty.
With
.BR \-f ,
an existing output file has already been deleted.
.TP
.B \-d
Decompress.
.TP
.B \-c
Write to standard output.
The output for several files is concatenated.
Input files are never deleted.
.TP
.B \-f
Replace existing output files, including read-only ones, and allow
reading from a console.
.TP
.B \-U
Delete each input file, including a read-only one, after processing it
successfully.
Ignored with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-k
Keep input files.
This is the default, and it overrides
.B \-U
wherever it appears.
.TP
.B \-t
Test: process each input and discard the output, creating and deleting
no files.
With
.BR \-d ,
this checks that the files decompress and that their checksums match.
Without
.BR \-d ,
.B "lzop \-t"
compresses and discards the result.
.TP
.B \-v
After writing each output file, print
\fIfile\fB: \fIN\fB% \- replaced with \fIoutput\fR
on standard error.
The percentage is meaningless, and the message appears even when the
input is kept.
Nothing is printed with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-t ,
or for standard input.
.TP
.B \-q
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-F
When compressing, store no block checksums, so
.B \-C
has no effect.
When decompressing, do not verify block checksums.
The header checksum is always stored and verified.
.TP
.B \-C
Also store an Adler-32 checksum of each compressed block, which is
verified when decompressing unless
.B \-F
is given.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
Recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the command line are expanded at startup, for callers such
as
.B cmd.exe
that do not expand them.
A busybox program started without this variable sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so the commands it runs do not expand their arguments again.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask applied at startup.
If it includes the owner write bit,
.BR 0200 ,
output files are created read-only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, or an error with any file.
.PP
A file that cannot be opened, or whose output file cannot be created,
is reported and skipped, and the remaining files are still processed.
Any other error, such as invalid input, a level from 7 to 9, or a write
error, stops
.B lzop
at once.
The incomplete output file is left in place, and the input file is not
deleted.
When compressing, a read error is taken as the end of the input, and is
not reported: the output is cut short, the status is 0, and with
.B \-U
the input file is deleted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compress a file, keeping the original:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lzop data.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Expand it, overwriting the existing
.IR data.bin :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unlzop \-f data.bin.lzo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a set of compressed files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lzop \-dt *.lzo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Archive a directory, and extract it again:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tar cf \- src | lzop >src.tar.lzo
$ lzopcat src.tar.lzo | tar xf \-
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
This
.B lzop
is derived from the original lzop and reads and writes the same format,
but it has fewer options, no levels above 6, and stores no file names,
permissions, or times.
.PP
Data is read and written in binary mode, with no newline translation.
Compressed data is written to a console without complaint.
.PP
The
.B .lzo
suffix is case-sensitive, so
.I DATA.LZO
expands to
.IR DATA.LZO.out .
The output of a read-only file is read-only.
.PP
When decompressing, blocks larger than 64 MiB are rejected as corrupted
data.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR bzip2 (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR xz (1),
.BR zstd (1)
